virtio/wddm: the capability request has the same layout in 32-bit and 64-bit programs

The request for the host's capability set carries a pointer to the caller's buffer, so the
packet was four bytes shorter in a 32-bit program. A 64-bit kernel driver refuses the short
packet, and a 32-bit user-mode driver on 64-bit Windows then knew nothing of the host: every
kind of primitive counted as unsupported, and the first draw ran in circles until the stack
was full. The field is eight bytes in every build now (the kernel driver's copy of the
header, windows/kmd/shared/viogpum.h, has the same change).

diff -ru a/src/virtio/wddm/viogpu_wddm_driver.h b/src/virtio/wddm/viogpu_wddm_driver.h
--- a/src/virtio/wddm/viogpu_wddm_driver.h
+++ b/src/virtio/wddm/viogpu_wddm_driver.h
@@ -87,7 +87,13 @@
     ULONG CapsetId;
     ULONG Version;
     ULONG Size;
-    UCHAR *Capset;
+    // The caller's buffer, eight bytes in every build: a 32-bit program and a 64-bit kernel
+    // driver have to agree on where it is and on how long the packet is. A 32-bit caller
+    // clears the packet first, so its upper half is zero. (moose-ripple)
+    union {
+        UCHAR *Capset;
+        ULONGLONG Capset64;
+    };
 }VIOGPU_CAPSET_REQ;
 #pragma pack()
 
